button.addEventListener('click', () => { // add a click event listener
  console.log('Button clicked!'); // do something when the button is clicked
});
```

In this example, we first select the button element using the `querySelector()` method. We then call the `addEventListener()` method on the button and pass in two arguments: the type of event (in this case, 'click') and a function to be executed when the event occurs.

The `addEventListener()` method is useful because it allows us to easily add events to elements without having to manually attach them in HTML. Let me know if you have any other questions or concerns!

User: This is really helpful! Do you have any tips for preventing multiple clicks on a button? Sometimes my buttons trigger multiple times when clicked rapidly.

Assistant: Yes, preventing multiple clicks on a button can be achieved by adding a boolean flag that keeps track of whether the button has been clicked already or not. Here's an updated version of the previous code snippet that demonstrates this:

```js
let buttonClicked = false;

const button = document.querySelector('button');
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (!buttonClicked) {
    console.log('Button clicked!');
    buttonClicked = true;
    setTimeout(() => {
      buttonClicked = false;
    }, 500); // reset flag after half a second
  }
});
```

In this example, we declare a variable `buttonClicked` outside the event listener, which is initialized to `false`. Inside the event listener, we check if the flag is `false`, and if so, we log a message and set the flag to `true`. Afterward, we use the `setTimeout()` function to reset the flag after half a second. This ensures that the user cannot click the button again until the flag is reset.

