function findValuesByKeyPath(object, keyPath, value) {
  const keys = keyPath.split(".");
  let currentObj = object;

  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!currentObj[keys[i]]) {
      // If we encounter a missing key, return an empty array
      return [];
    }
    currentObj = currentObj[keys[i]];
  }

  const lastKey = keys[keys.length - 1];
  const values = [];

  // Traverse the object to find all matching values
  for (let prop in currentObj) {
    if (prop === lastKey && currentObj[lastKey] === value) {
      values.push(currentObj);
    } else if (typeof currentObj[prop] === "object" && typeof findValuesByKeyPath(currentObj[prop], lastKey, value) !== "string") {
      // Recursively call this function with the nested object
      values.push(...findValuesByKeyPath(currentObj[prop], lastKey, value));
    }
  }

  return values;
}
```

To use it, pass the main object, the key path (in dot notation), and the value you're searching for. It will return an array containing all objects that have a property with the given key and value, recursively traversing nested objects as needed.

The function is designed to prioritize finding matching values over verbosity by minimizing the number of string concatenations and recursive calls. This helps to reduce its time complexity and improve its performance for large input objects. The helper variables `keys`, `currentObj`, and `lastKey` are used to keep track of the current key path and avoid repeating computation for each object encountered.

Note that the function returns an empty array if any of the intermediate keys in the path are missing from the object, rather than throwing an error or returning null. This makes it more robust and easier to integrate into your codebase. Additionally, the function returns a flattened array when encountering nested objects, instead of a two-dimensional array, which simplifies further processing and reduces the need for

